Thoughts & Architectural Suggestions
Your framing hits three strong angles: AI systems development, model evaluation, and human-in-the-loop failure analysis. Framing yourself as an independent builder who ships tools and diagnoses model failure modes—rather than a generic prompt writer—gives you stronger leverage for technical evaluation, research participation, and early-access programs while keeping Sweden eligibility prioritized and international/remote options visible.
Practical Architecture for GitHub Pages + GitHub Actions + SPA PWA
 * Split static hosting from scheduled discovery: GitHub Pages serves static files only. Use a GitHub Actions scheduled workflow (schedule cron + workflow_dispatch) to fetch/normalize opportunities, run deterministic scoring, and publish a static data/opportunities.json file (either committed to the repo or artifact-deployed with the PWA).
 * Client-side state with IndexedDB + localStorage: Keep personal profile data, custom scoring weights, application stages, notes, and dismissed/saved states in browser storage (IndexedDB with localStorage fallback), plus one-click JSON export/import. No external database is needed for a personal v1.
 * Alerts without a custom server: Use GitHub Actions to open/update a GitHub Issue or send a webhook/email digest when high-match items or closing deadlines appear. Add Web Push later once core triage works.
 * PRD cleanup suggestions:
   * Unify the scoring weights between the notes and PRD sections (use one deterministic baseline formula with explicit eligibility status so ineligible items never masquerade as top matches).
   * Keep external web text strictly as untrusted data (never execute instructions found inside scraped descriptions).
   * Ship a working manual + sample-seeded + JSON-ingestable SPA PWA first, then wire live GitHub Actions feeds incrementally.
